              {
                "id": "R4-deadcode-gate-fetch",
                "lens": "resilience",
                "location": ".github/workflows/ci.yml:109",
                "severity": "WARNING",
                "claim": "The engine and tui Deadcode steps changed from informational (continue-on-error, errors swallowed) to blocking, and they still fetch deadcode with go run at @v0.48.0 on every run. A module proxy outage or a failed toolchain switch now fails the job. The engine job sets up Go 1.21 and relies on automatic toolchain switching, and the task log says this is not provable locally and that the first CI run is the proof. The failure is loud rather than silent, and the staticcheck step already depends on the same mechanism, so this is a residual risk rather than a demonstrated defect.",
                "proof_refs": [
                  ".github/workflows/ci.yml:109",
                  "odd/tasks/role-agents-slice6.md:28"
                ],
                "evidence_class": "inferential",
                "causal_disposition": "worsened"
              }
            ],
            "evidence": [
              "Reviewed all 8 changed paths in full.",
              "The CI gate uses command substitution under bash -e, so a go run failure (version unresolvable, type error) exits non-zero and fails the step. Findings are echoed and then fail the step with an ::error annotation, so failures are observable.",
              "The deleted functions (ProjectionHookEntries, BaselineLintDecision) are reached only by deadcode -test reports per the task log. The one caller of BaselineLintDecision's replacement already uses BaselineLintDecisionAgainst, and only a comment changed in approve.go.",
              "The new manifest test uses t.TempDir for destinations and only stats files, so it has no retry or cleanup hazard.",
              "The tui tag-guard changes only add a deadcode case to the test reader classifier and require a deadcode step in the TUI job, so the guard is stricter without changing runtime behavior."
            ]
          }
        }
